WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.310
Authentication and authorization are related, but they answer different questions.

2
00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.147
Authentication asks who the user or caller is.

3
00:00:08.147 --> 00:00:11.420
Authorization asks what that caller is allowed to do.

4
00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:19.639
A TypeScript type can model a user, role, claim, tenant, or request context, but the type alone does not prove that an action is allowed.

5
00:00:19.639 --> 00:00:23.422
Sensitive checks should happen in trusted server-side code.

6
00:00:23.422 --> 00:00:32.223
API handlers, service functions, middleware, and domain services should verify that the current caller can perform the requested operation.

7
00:00:32.223 --> 00:00:40.734
This includes route-level checks, object-level permission checks, tenant boundaries, ownership rules, and capability requirements.

8
00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:45.389
It is not enough for the frontend to hide a button or mark a route as protected.

9
00:00:45.389 --> 00:00:49.026
Authorization logic should be consistent and reviewable.

10
00:00:49.026 --> 00:00:54.118
Scattered checks make it harder to know whether every sensitive path is protected.

11
00:00:54.118 --> 00:01:03.283
Many applications benefit from shared helpers, policy functions, middleware, or service-layer methods that make permission decisions explicit.

12
00:01:03.283 --> 00:01:09.611
The right structure depends on the framework, but the security goal is the same: avoid surprising gaps.

13
00:01:09.611 --> 00:01:13.612
Session-aware code should avoid assumptions that are too broad.

14
00:01:13.612 --> 00:01:17.176
A logged-in user may not be allowed to access every object.

15
00:01:17.176 --> 00:01:21.176
An administrator in one tenant may not have authority in another.

16
00:01:21.176 --> 00:01:25.977
A background job may have different permissions than an interactive request.

17
00:01:25.977 --> 00:01:33.542
Deny-by-default behavior, least privilege, and tests for access-control edge cases help keep those assumptions honest.

18
00:01:33.542 --> 00:01:42.925
TypeScript can make these checks easier to review by modeling request contexts, authenticated principals, roles, capabilities, and resource owners.

19
00:01:42.925 --> 00:01:47.144
Those models are helpful only when they are tied to real enforcement.

20
00:01:47.144 --> 00:01:58.637
Sensitive operations should make the required permission visible at the call site or service boundary, so reviewers can see both the action and the rule protecting it before data changes.
